      "answer": "The enumeration confirms the vulnerability. Here's what happened:\n\n**Result:** IDs 1001 and 1002 return orders belonging to \"you\", but **id 1041** returns a different user's order \u2014 `r.mensah` \u2014 with a 200 response and no authorization check.\n\n**Order at id 1041 (not yours):**\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": 1041,\n  \"user\": \"r.mensah\",\n  \"item\": \"The Ridge\",\n  \"total\": \"22.00\",\n  \"note\": \"gift message: zidor-4417-note\"\n}\n```\n\n**Finding:** The `/api/orders/{id}` endpoint does **not enforce authorization**. By guessing numeric IDs, I can access any user's order without authentication or permission checks \u2014 a classic IDOR (Insecure Direct Object Reference) vulnerability.",
